[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a minimal, copy-and-paste-ready example that shows the three things you need to do:

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (the DEM tiles you want to shade).  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that points at that source.  
3. (Optional) tweak the hillshade paint properties to get the look you want.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣  Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // 2️⃣  Raster-DEM source (replace the URL template with your own)
        "my-dem": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          // Example: MapLibre demo tiles (public, no API key)
          // You can also point at your own tiles (e.g. /tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png)
          url: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
          tileSize: 256,
          // DEMs are usually 0-255 elevation values; set the correct encoding
          // (MapLibre defaults to "mapbox" which expects 0-255 → 0-65535m)
          // If you use a different encoding, set `encoding: "terrarium"` or
          // `encoding: "mapbox"` accordingly.
          encoding: "mapbox"
        },

        // A simple basemap so you can see the hillshade on top of something
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          url: "https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        }
      },

      // 3️⃣  Layers – first the basemap, then the hillshade
      layers: [
        {
          id: "osm-basemap",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        },
        {
          id: "hillshade",
          type: "hillshade",
          source: "my-dem",
          // The source-layer property is **not** used for raster-dem sources.
          // All you need is the source name.
          paint: {
            // Default values are shown; adjust to taste.
            "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315, // degrees clockwise from north
            "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "viewport", // or "map"
            "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.5, // 0-1 makes the effect subtler
            "hillshade-shadow-color": "#000000",
            "hillshade-shadow-color-transition": {duration: 0},
            "hillshade-highlight-color": "#ffffff",
            "hillshade-accent-color": "#777777"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 45,   // tilt the map so the shading is visible
    bearing: -17 // optional rotation
  });

  // Optional: add navigation controls
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | What you need to provide | Important notes |
|------|--------------------------|-----------------|
| **Raster-DEM source** | `type: "raster-dem"`<br>`url` (or `tiles` array) with `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders<br>`tileSize` (usually 256 or 512)<br>`encoding` (`"mapbox"` or `"terrarium"` depending on how the DEM was generated) | The source **must** be `raster-dem`. Vector sources cannot be used for hillshade. |
| **Hillshade layer** | `type: "hillshade"`<br>`source` = name of the DEM source<br>Optional `paint` properties to control shading | No `source-layer` is required. The layer automatically reads elevation values from the DEM tiles. |
| **Map pitch & bearing** | Set `pitch` > 0 (e.g., 45°) so the light direction creates visible shadows. | Without pitch the hillshade looks flat because the illumination is orthogonal to the screen. |

### Common tweaks & troubleshooting

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **No shading appears** | • Make sure the DEM tiles are reachable (check network tab). <br>• Verify `encoding` matches the DEM format (`mapbox` for Mapbox-style PNGs, `terrarium` for Terrarium PNGs). <br>• Set a non-zero `hillshade-exaggeration` (default 1 works, but you can lower it for subtlety). |
| **Shading looks too strong / too weak** | Adjust `hillshade-exaggeration` (0 – 1) or change the illumination direction (`hillshade-illumination-direction`). |
| **CORS errors** | Serve the DEM tiles with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). MapLibre cannot read tiles that are blocked by CORS. |
| **Using your own DEM tiles** | Replace the `url` template with the path to your tiles (e.g., `https://myserver.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png`). If you host them on the same origin as the page, you can omit CORS headers. |
| **Want the hillshade on top of a vector basemap** | Add the hillshade layer **after** the vector layers you want it to overlay, or give it a higher `z-index` by ordering it later in the `layers` array. |
| **Changing the light source at runtime** | Call `map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-illumination-direction', newDegrees);` (or any other paint property). |

### Minimal checklist before you go live

1. **DEM source** – `type: "raster-dem"` and correct `encoding`.  
2. **Hillshade layer** – `type: "hillshade"` referencing that source.  
3. **Map pitch** – > 0 (usually 45°).  
4. **CORS** – DEM tiles must be publicly fetchable.  
5. **Version** – MapLibre GL JS ≥ 1.0 (the example uses 4.3.0, the latest at time of writing).  

That’s all you need to get a hillshade layer working from a raster-DEM source in a MapLibre GL JS style. Happy mapping!
